From 91bfee11a10a0546be7a2e5594c2cc892dc2dd04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 17:49:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Static subtrees: a placed block's parts leave three's
 per-frame matrix walk

scene.updateMatrixWorld() composes and multiplies the matrices of every object every frame, moved or not: on
the street that is ~24,000 objects, 14,400 of them inside the placed blocks (parked cars with their 15-25
parts, sidewalk models, lamp posts, merged building meshes), and none of those moves. It was 4-5 ms of a
frame's JS (46 % of render() under fastrender on an M2 Air).

src/static.ts: freeze(o) computes a subtree's matrices once and takes it out of the walk; staticContainer(g)
looks at g's frozen children only when g really moved. NycLevel.place() freezes every part of a block but the
steam puffs. Kept exact without the callers' help: children added below a frozen object (models that stream
in, a car's near LOD, far-LOD twins: Object3D.add is wrapped), the object's own position / quaternion / scale,
any ancestor's move (windowview.ts shifts the level), three's explicit updateMatrixWorld(true) /
updateWorldMatrix(). A transform written by hand below a frozen object needs touch(o); nothing in the game
does that to a block today, and StaticAudit re-checks ~256 frozen objects per frame as a net (recomputes,
warns once, thaws a subtree the third time).

STATIC.on (globalThis.__staticFreeze, ?static=0) switches it off at once for comparisons.

Node run of the game through fastrender (spawn view, interleaved): scene.updateMatrixWorld 4.1-4.6 -> 0.7-0.9
ms, render() 12.9-13.9 -> 7.3-8.0 ms; every matrix equal to three's plain walk on every frame checked, also
while walking 600 m through streaming blocks. tests/static.test.ts.
